\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{From the six-cycle to the three-cycle}
\date{2026-11-06}

\p{Take #{q=2}, #{N=3}, diagonal value #{a=3} and neighbor value
#{b=1}. The fine cycle has 6 vertices and shape
#{t=3/2}. The polynomials of \ref{fdip-00B1} give
#{T_2(3/2)=7/2} and #{U_1(3/2)=3}, so the retained precision
of \ref{fdip-00B3} on the three kept vertices has neighbor value
#{b'=1/3}, diagonal
value #{a'=7/3}, and shape #{7/2}. Each eliminated path is a
single vertex of determinant #{3}, and the three paths
contribute determinant #{27}. The Gaussian factor of the
eliminated block is #{(2\pi / 3)^{3/2}}.}

\p{The same matrix sits in \ref{fdip-0057}. There the neighbor
value is #{1} and the diagonal is #{2} plus the mass parameter.
With mass parameter #{1} the diagonal is #{3}. The retained
weight is #{1/3} and the retained mass parameter is #{5/3}.
The matrix of that retained precision has diagonal
#{2\cdot(1/3)+5/3=7/3} and off-diagonal #{-1/3}, which is the
pair #{(a',b')} above. The picture in that example is this
six-cycle.}
